Cervicalgia (Mechanical and Spondylotic Neck Pain)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Posterior neck ache, Exacerbation with static posture, Paraspinal muscle spasms, Absence of radiculopathy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-012
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of posterior neck pain with extreme muscle spasm limiting all head movement.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with acute, severe neck stiffness and an inability to rotate the head, complicated by a severe secondary headache. Urgent clinical assessment was strictly necessary to rule out acute meningitis, subarachnoid hemorrhage, or severe cervical radiculopathy, and to provide potent, fast-acting muscle relaxants and analgesia to break the mechanical spasm cycle prior to daytime services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My neck is completely locked, and the tension is shooting a blinding headache right over the top of my skull."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute flare of mechanical cervicalgia following prolonged desk work. Timeline: Insidious onset of dull, aching pain localized to the posterior neck and upper trapezius over the last 48 hours; rapidly progressed 6 hours ago into severe, unyielding muscle spasms. Currently reports extreme subjective neck stiffness and a near-total restriction of active cervical range of motion. Pain is excruciating at the extremes of attempted movement. Patient reports a secondary, tight, band-like headache originating from the cervicocranial junction (mimicking a tension-type headache).
Functional Status: Immobilized upper body; patient rotates entire torso to look around the room.
Social/Safety Concerns: Unable to safely drive or operate machinery due to absent cervical rotation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/88 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, in moderate distress, sitting rigidly with the head held in slight forward flexion.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination of the upper limbs reveals 5/5 motor strength symmetrically, intact sensation to light touch/pinprick in all dermatomes, and 2+ symmetrical biceps/triceps/brachioradialis reflexes. Distinct absence of objective radicular features. Severe, palpable muscle spasm, tension, and active trigger points located in the bilateral upper trapezius, levator scapulae, and paraspinal musculature. Range of motion heavily restricted: rotation <30 degrees bilaterally, extension <10 degrees, limited entirely by mechanical muscular pain. Negative Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs. No photophobia.
Environmental Observations: Evidence of poor ergonomic workstation setup in the living area.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol 1g and Ibuprofen 400mg taken 4 hours ago with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Mechanical Cervicalgia with Cervicogenic Headache - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Meningitis - Ruled out clinically by the absence of fever, negative meningeal signs (Kernig/Brudzinski), and lack of systemic toxicity or photophobia.

Cervical Radiculopathy - Ruled out by completely normal and symmetrical upper limb neurovascular examination and absence of radiating arm pain.

Subarachnoid Hemorrhage - Ruled out by the gradual, progressive mechanical onset over 48 hours rather than a sudden "thunderclap" presentation, and the clear musculoskeletal provocation of the headache.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, immobilizing mechanical neck spasm complicated by a cervicogenic headache, completely restricting cervical mobility. The crucial clinical step was to rapidly differentiate this extreme musculoskeletal tension from life-threatening intracranial or infectious central nervous system pathology. Once meningitis and intracranial hemorrhage were definitively excluded through a normal neurological and systemic examination, the immediate clinical need was to relieve the intense, unyielding muscle spasm. Delaying treatment until regular hours would have left the patient in profound discomfort, incapable of sleep, and functionally paralyzed. Urgent after-hours intervention safely provided the necessary pharmacological blockade to break the spasm cycle.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ketorolac 30mg STAT. Prescribed oral Diazepam 2mg BD PRN for 3 days to break the acute muscle spasm.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Apply a hot pack to the posterior neck and upper shoulders for 20 minutes before attempting any movement. Consequence: Increased local blood flow and softening of the tight muscle fibers, making the first turns of the head significantly easier and less painful (positive reinforcement).
Action: Set a strict 30-minute timer when doing any desk work or screen time, using the time to stand and perform three slow shoulder shrugs. Consequence: Prevention of the static postural fatigue that triggers the severe locking spasms (negative reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral for ergonomic assessment and postural strengthening.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A. (Strict advice not to drive).
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days if symptoms do not fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is sudden onset of fever, photophobia, an explosive "worst headache of life", or new weakness/numbness radiating down either arm.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical assessment of mechanical joint/muscle pain. X-rays are not initially indicated without "red flag" symptoms. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute spasm will usually "unlock" within 48-72 hours with muscle relaxants and heat. Long-term prevention requires permanent changes to physical ergonomics and posture.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care immediately if you develop a fever, if bright lights hurt your eyes, or if you feel pain or numbness shooting down into your arms or hands.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The muscles in your neck have essentially gone into a defensive cramp due to poor posture. The injected anti-inflammatory reduces the pain, while the short course of Valium acts directly on the nerves to command the muscles to let go.
Actionable Steps: Use heat, not ice. Wrap a hot towel or heat pack around your neck to melt the tension. Do not force your neck to move past the point of pain; let the medications do the work first.

SMS INFORMATION

